{
  "enabled": true,
  "name": "Reflect After Task",
  "description": "After completing a task, fold anything newly learned into the steering file that owns the topic as an affirmative statement, correcting wrong lines in place. Does not append to a journal.",
  "version": "1",
  "when": {
    "type": "stop"
  },
  "then": {
    "type": "askAgent",
    "prompt": "Now that this task is complete, reflect on the work you just did:\n\n1. **What was new or surprising?** Did you encounter any constraint, behaviour or pattern that the documentation does not already cover?\n2. **What was incorrect?** Did any documentation, steering file or code comment turn out to be wrong or misleading?\n3. **What generalises?** Only keep what would apply to ANY future container build, not to the thing you just built.\n\nIf nothing generalises, say so and stop. Do not manufacture a lesson to have something to write.\n\nOtherwise, write it into the file that OWNS the topic, and nowhere else:\n\n- `.kiro/steering/container-platform-limits.md` — platform facts: compose rules, networking, volumes, health checks, memory and image sizes, Config Store and `cs.sock` behaviour, log retrieval, capabilities. This is the authoritative fact file.\n- `.kiro/steering/container-building.md` — the workflow, phases, conventions and reference-sample descriptions.\n- `docs/` — detail, worked examples and reference material. `container-development-guide.md`, `cs-sock-protocol.md`, `ncos-sdk-reference.md`, `memory-resources.md`.\n- Code comments and docstrings — when the wrong claim lives in code. A false explanation inside code is read as a runtime observation, so it is worse than one in a doc.\n\n**Do NOT append a dated entry anywhere. Do NOT write to `.kiro/steering/lessons-learned.md` — it is a closed archive.**\n\nHow to write it:\n\n- **Correct wrong lines in place.** Edit the sentence that is wrong rather than adding a contradicting one below it, then re-read the surrounding prose so the file does not argue with itself. Grep for the CLAIM across `.kiro/steering/`, `docs/`, `README.md` and code, not just the file you noticed it in, and fix every copy — including places that merely rely on the claim without stating it.\n- **State facts affirmatively, in present tense.** \"The engine attaches the syslog driver, so `container logs` returns nothing; read the router log with `log show -i -s <name>`.\" Not \"we discovered that...\" or \"it seems that...\".\n- **Include negative constraints with their reason and the alternative**, in the form \"X does not work because Y, do Z instead\", so the constraint does not get re-derived by the next session.\n- **Mark anything untested `UNVERIFIED` inline, next to the claim it qualifies** — not in a footnote or a separate section. An unmarked claim is read as established fact.\n- **Record the device model and NCOS firmware version alongside any on-router observation.** Without them the result cannot be scoped and has to be re-run on the next device. If you do not have them, say explicitly that they were not captured.\n- **Keep it dense.** One or two lines per fact. These files load into every session, so length is a real cost. Prefer editing an existing bullet over adding a near-duplicate.\n- **When a claim's status changes from open to settled, sweep for every place that framed it as open** and update those too. Search for the concept — headings, synonyms, a distinctive phrase from the argument — not just the identifier, or the sweep returns a false all-clear.\n- **Verify every cross-reference you write.** After writing \"documented in X\", grep X for the thing. A confident pointer to something that is not there is worse than no pointer.\n\nFinally, report which files you changed and which claims you corrected."
  }
}
